course: dictionary
lesson: 10882
title: Vinegar: sour, useful and fizzy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have clues all around it. Read the sentence and ask what kind of thing vinegar is. «Salt and vinegar on chips» tells you it is something you eat with food, so it is a flavouring.
explain: «The acid in the vinegar and the alkali in the soda react and make a new substance, carbon dioxide gas, which bubbles out.» The clues are acid, react and bubbles, so vinegar is the sour part of a fizzy science experiment. Check by swapping in your meaning: "The sour liquid in the vinegar and the soda react" still makes sense.
explain: The same word can be used in different places. In the kitchen vinegar flavours food, in science it fizzes, and at home «vinegar removes limescale» so it is used for cleaning. Look at the topic of the sentence to find which job the word is doing.
fact: Vinegar is a sour liquid made from wine, cider or malt.
fact: Vinegar is an acid, so it fizzes when it meets bicarbonate of soda.
fact: When vinegar and bicarbonate of soda react, they make carbon dioxide gas, which bubbles out.
fact: Vinegar is used to flavour food, to keep food such as pickles, and to clean things such as limescale.
fact: Vinegar is an uncountable noun, so we say «some vinegar», but vinegars means different kinds.
exercise: «Salt and vinegar on chips» shows that vinegar is used for what? | Cleaning windows | *Flavouring food | Keeping a fire going | Making bread rise
exercise: Sami mixes vinegar and bicarbonate of soda, and it fizzes. What does the fizz show? | Nothing has happened | The vinegar has turned into water | *A new gas, carbon dioxide, is made | The soda has gone cold
exercise: «Vinegar with a pinch of salt makes a dull coin shiny again.» Find and copy the word that means not bright. | shiny | coin | again | *dull
test: «Lifeguards in Australia pour vinegar on the stings of box jellyfish to stop the unfired cells.» Why do they use vinegar? | To make the jellyfish sweeter | *To stop the unfired cells from firing | To clean the sand | To freeze the water
test: Vinegar is sour. What does sour mean? | Hot like pepper | *Sharp tasting | Sweet and sticky | Cold like ice
exam: What is vinegar made from? | Sugar and flour | Salt and water | *Wine, cider or malt | Milk and cream
